WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:03.240
<v Lecturer>Okay, so now let's go over the VPC section,</v>

2
00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:05.790
and first I want to remind you of some basics.

3
00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:07.170
Now if you don't remember them all,

4
00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:09.330
I'll suggest you take back one of my courses

5
00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:11.520
and look at the VPC section, but in here,

6
00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:14.160
I would assume you know all the basics of VPC

7
00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:16.110
that I'm going to go over right now.

8
00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:19.200
If anything is unclear, please investigate on your own.

9
00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:22.350
So a CIDR is a block of IP addresses,

10
00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:25.470
and is defined as an IP address slash a number,

11
00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:27.690
and the number defines the IP range.

12
00:00:27.690 --> 00:00:32.670
So for this example, 192.168.0.0/26

13
00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:36.270
is equivalent to the range of 64 IP

14
00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:38.670
that start at 192 168 0 0,

15
00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:42.960
all the way to 192 168 0 63.

16
00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:45.633
The CIDR block are going to be used everywhere on AWS,

17
00:00:45.633 --> 00:00:48.060
they will be used when you have security groups,

18
00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:52.410
root tables, VPC, subnets, et cetera, et cetera.

19
00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:53.850
So knowing what a CIDR block is,

20
00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:56.880
is very important going into the exam obviously.

21
00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:59.400
A private IP is going to be an IP

22
00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:00.870
that's going to be only accessible

23
00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:04.050
within a private network, and there is a limited range

24
00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:06.720
of private IP that is defined by the norms,

25
00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:10.410
so we have the range from 10 0 0 0, all the way

26
00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:14.300
to 10 255 255 255, which represents the CIDR block

27
00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:18.810
10 0 0 0/8, which is when you have a big network.

28
00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:23.810
Then there is the 172.16.0.0 all the way to 172 31 255 255.

29
00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:29.550
And finally we have the smaller 192 168 0 0

30
00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:32.400
all the way to 192 168 255 255,

31
00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:35.070
which represents the home network, for example,

32
00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:37.980
the kind of things you have using your home router.

33
00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:41.550
So these are the only possible private IP you have.

34
00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:44.430
Anything else is going to be a public IP.

35
00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:48.150
So this is all you need to know right now on the IP side.

36
00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:49.560
Next we have the VPC.

37
00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:50.473
What is a VPC?

38
00:01:50.473 --> 00:01:54.900
A VPC must be defined as a list of CIDR blocks

39
00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:57.450
and cannot be changed, and the VPC will represent

40
00:01:57.450 --> 00:02:02.250
your virtual private cloud, based on these CIDR blocks.

41
00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:05.910
So each CIDR within your VPC must have a minimum size

42
00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:09.840
of /28 and a maximum size of /16,

43
00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:14.010
which represents 65536 IP addresses.

44
00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:16.320
The VPC is by definition private,

45
00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:19.200
so only private IP CIDR range are going

46
00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:21.273
to be allowed within your VPC.

47
00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:26.550
Next, a VPC is made of subnets, so this is within the VPC,

48
00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:28.350
and you define, again, a CIDR that is going

49
00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:31.140
to be a subset of the VPC CIDR,

50
00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:33.210
that's why it's called a subnet.

51
00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:35.100
All the instances within the subnet

52
00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:38.610
will get a private IP based on the CIDR you have defined

53
00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:39.930
for that subnet.

54
00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:43.080
And the first four IP and the last one in every subnet

55
00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:47.280
are going to be reserved by AWS for networking purposes.

56
00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:49.020
Okay, then we have route tables.

57
00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:51.750
Route tables are used to control where the network traffic

58
00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:54.450
will be directed to, and they're associated

59
00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:56.010
with specific subnets.

60
00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:57.990
They're an idea that to say, okay,

61
00:02:57.990 --> 00:03:00.270
if we're going from one subnet to another,

62
00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:01.650
you need to use this route.

63
00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:03.540
This is what the route tables are used for.

64
00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:06.630
The most specific routing rule is always followed,

65
00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:10.140
so as long as the routing rule is going to be smaller,

66
00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:12.510
so the slash number is going to be higher,

67
00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:15.270
then this route will always be followed by default.

68
00:03:15.270 --> 00:03:16.980
And this is something we'll have a look at

69
00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:19.189
when we'll look at VPC peering.

70
00:03:19.189 --> 00:03:22.650
Okay. Next, we have internet gateway or IGW,

71
00:03:22.650 --> 00:03:25.530
to help connect our VPC to the internet.

72
00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:29.490
It's highly available and it will scale horizontally.

73
00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:32.760
The idea is that it will act as a net for instances

74
00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:36.810
that have a public IPv4 or a public IPv6.

75
00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:40.350
So any instance that you linked to your internet gateway

76
00:03:40.350 --> 00:03:42.270
will have internet connectivity,

77
00:03:42.270 --> 00:03:44.820
and this is how we define public subnets.

78
00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:47.580
So public subnets are subnets that will have a route

79
00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:51.720
that will send 0 0 0/0, so that means all internet traffic

80
00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:55.500
to an internet gateway, and the instances must have

81
00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:58.350
a public IPv4 to talk to the internet,

82
00:03:58.350 --> 00:04:00.210
that's where they're called public subnets

83
00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:01.830
and public instances.

84
00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:05.220
Whereas private subnets are accessing the internet

85
00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:08.490
through a NAT Instance or a NAT Gateway,

86
00:04:08.490 --> 00:04:10.740
and that NAT Instance or that NAT Gateway

87
00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:13.530
needs to be set up in a public subnet.

88
00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:16.110
And then the routes on the private subnet

89
00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:20.070
must be so that 0.0.0/0 routes all the traffic

90
00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:23.310
to either your NAT Instance or your NAT Gateway.

91
00:04:23.310 --> 00:04:26.310
So this is the idea, internet gateway, public subnets,

92
00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:28.350
private subnets, NAT Instance, NAT Gateway,

93
00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:29.820
you should know this already.

94
00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:31.710
So next, we have NAT Instances.

95
00:04:31.710 --> 00:04:34.140
So there are EC2 instances that you deploy

96
00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:36.150
in a public subnet, and the way it works

97
00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:39.180
that we edit our route table so that our private subnet

98
00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:43.830
has to route for the internet through the NAT Instance.

99
00:04:43.830 --> 00:04:44.850
So it's not resilient to failure

100
00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:46.470
because we only have one instance,

101
00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:48.120
we have limited bandwidth because it's based

102
00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:50.760
on the instance type, but it's a cheap solution.

103
00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:52.110
And in case of any failover,

104
00:04:52.110 --> 00:04:53.850
it must be managed by ourselves,

105
00:04:53.850 --> 00:04:55.980
and for NAT Instance to work,

106
00:04:55.980 --> 00:05:00.270
we need to disable the source destination check settings

107
00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:02.880
on EC2, and that is the case with any type

108
00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:05.820
of instance that proxies internet traffic.

109
00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:07.110
So the architecture is at such

110
00:05:07.110 --> 00:05:10.500
where we have a public and a private subnet,

111
00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:13.470
and in the private subnet we have an EC2 instance,

112
00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:15.030
and then in the public subnet,

113
00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:17.460
we're going to create a NAT Instance.

114
00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:19.110
Now, thanks to the route table,

115
00:05:19.110 --> 00:05:21.660
the private EC2 instance traffic to the internet

116
00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:24.390
is going to go through the NAT Instance,

117
00:05:24.390 --> 00:05:26.310
and then the NAT Instance is going to direct

118
00:05:26.310 --> 00:05:28.290
to the public internet.

119
00:05:28.290 --> 00:05:31.020
The NAT Instance should be assigned an elastic IP,

120
00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:34.110
and therefore the public internet will see all the traffic

121
00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:36.900
originating from that one elastic IP.

122
00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:40.440
So remember, the source check must be disabled.

123
00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:41.820
And so therefore, because we only have

124
00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:44.940
one public IP, then if we're trying to access

125
00:05:44.940 --> 00:05:47.460
a third party service, we can tell them to whitelist

126
00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:49.980
a specific elastic IP to make sure

127
00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:52.773
that there is some kind of network security in there.

128
00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:55.920
For NAT Gateways, it's a managed solution,

129
00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:58.320
so in this case, the NAT solution is managed

130
00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:00.510
and the bandwidth increases over time.

131
00:06:00.510 --> 00:06:03.330
It's resilience to failures within a single AZ,

132
00:06:03.330 --> 00:06:06.990
and if you want to have management of failures across AZ

133
00:06:06.990 --> 00:06:08.760
then you can deploy the NAT Gateway

134
00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:10.950
in multiple availability zones.

135
00:06:10.950 --> 00:06:15.600
So you can also assign an elastic IP to each NAT Gateway

136
00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:17.547
and therefore whenever we access the internet,

137
00:06:17.547 --> 00:06:20.070
the traffic will be seen as originating

138
00:06:20.070 --> 00:06:22.260
from that specific elastic IP.

139
00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:25.080
So we have an architecture here with two AZs,

140
00:06:25.080 --> 00:06:27.270
two private subnets, two public subnets,

141
00:06:27.270 --> 00:06:30.840
we're going to create a NAT Gateway in each public subnet

142
00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:33.060
and each will have its own elastic IP.

143
00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:35.760
And so therefore the EC2 instance can be routed

144
00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:39.150
through the NAT Gateway to the internet gateway and so on,

145
00:06:39.150 --> 00:06:41.820
for AZ-B, and we have a highly available kind

146
00:06:41.820 --> 00:06:45.100
of setup across multiple AZs.

147
00:06:45.100 --> 00:06:47.850
Okay, next we have network ACLs or NACL.

148
00:06:47.850 --> 00:06:51.000
They're stateless firewalls defined at the subnet level

149
00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:54.390
and they will apply to all the instances within that subnet,

150
00:06:54.390 --> 00:06:56.100
and NACLs are special because they allow

151
00:06:56.100 --> 00:06:59.130
for allow and deny rules.

152
00:06:59.130 --> 00:07:00.480
Stateless, what does that means?

153
00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:02.610
That means that any return traffic

154
00:07:02.610 --> 00:07:05.280
must be explicitly allowed by the rules.

155
00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:07.470
So this is very helpful when you want to quickly

156
00:07:07.470 --> 00:07:09.930
and cheaply block a specific IP address,

157
00:07:09.930 --> 00:07:12.360
but they're complicated to use because there are stateless

158
00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:15.150
and so you need to have both rules allowing traffic in

159
00:07:15.150 --> 00:07:17.310
and allowing traffic out.

160
00:07:17.310 --> 00:07:20.160
Then we have security groups, and these ones are applied

161
00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:23.250
at the instance level, not the subnet level,

162
00:07:23.250 --> 00:07:25.980
and they only allow for allow rules, okay?

163
00:07:25.980 --> 00:07:27.900
They don't have any deny rules.

164
00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:29.970
Stateful means that the return traffic

165
00:07:29.970 --> 00:07:33.390
is automatically allowed, regardless of what the rule says,

166
00:07:33.390 --> 00:07:35.640
so that means that if your traffic is,

167
00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:37.110
if your instance is sending traffic

168
00:07:37.110 --> 00:07:39.720
to a third party service, you have the guarantee

169
00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:41.430
that it will be able to come back

170
00:07:41.430 --> 00:07:44.040
through the security group, obviously,

171
00:07:44.040 --> 00:07:46.170
if the network ACL allows it as well,

172
00:07:46.170 --> 00:07:48.360
and traffic going into your instance,

173
00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:50.280
for example if you're having HTP server,

174
00:07:50.280 --> 00:07:53.880
is allowed to go back out to the service

175
00:07:53.880 --> 00:07:55.650
that requested stuff from you.

176
00:07:55.650 --> 00:07:57.810
Okay. Security groups are very special

177
00:07:57.810 --> 00:07:59.820
because they also allow you to reference

178
00:07:59.820 --> 00:08:02.430
other security groups in the same region,

179
00:08:02.430 --> 00:08:04.530
whether it be even with a peered VPC

180
00:08:04.530 --> 00:08:07.022
or with cross accounts.

181
00:08:07.022 --> 00:08:08.670
Okay. Next, how about logging?

182
00:08:08.670 --> 00:08:11.610
So we have VPC flow logs, and they allow you

183
00:08:11.610 --> 00:08:15.300
to log the internet traffic going through your VPC.

184
00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:17.850
And you can have three kind of VPC flow logs,

185
00:08:17.850 --> 00:08:20.160
the first one can be defined at the VPC level,

186
00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:21.870
the second one is a subnet level,

187
00:08:21.870 --> 00:08:24.960
or third as the ENI level, elastic network interface,

188
00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:26.910
which is the virtual network card

189
00:08:26.910 --> 00:08:29.430
attached usually to your EC2 instances.

190
00:08:29.430 --> 00:08:31.620
They're very helpful when you want to understand

191
00:08:31.620 --> 00:08:34.020
why your traffic is not going the way it's wanted,

192
00:08:34.020 --> 00:08:36.480
so to capture denied internet traffic,

193
00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:39.180
this will show you why it failed and where it failed.

194
00:08:39.180 --> 00:08:41.700
And then these VPC flow logs can be sent

195
00:08:41.700 --> 00:08:43.470
to CloudWatch Logs or Amazon S3,

196
00:08:43.470 --> 00:08:45.900
which is very helpful if you want to see, for example,

197
00:08:45.900 --> 00:08:48.960
an increase in traffic onto your infrastructure,

198
00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:50.850
and that will probably lead to your thinking

199
00:08:50.850 --> 00:08:52.680
that you're under attack.

200
00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:54.270
Next, we have Bastion Hosts,

201
00:08:54.270 --> 00:08:56.580
and so they allow you to do SSH

202
00:08:56.580 --> 00:08:59.790
into private EC2 instances by using a bastion host

203
00:08:59.790 --> 00:09:02.730
which is a public EC2 instance in the public subnets.

204
00:09:02.730 --> 00:09:05.100
So it's a two hop SSH.

205
00:09:05.100 --> 00:09:07.170
And in case you're using bastion host

206
00:09:07.170 --> 00:09:08.760
you must manage the instances yourself,

207
00:09:08.760 --> 00:09:11.220
there is no managed bastion host by AWS.

208
00:09:11.220 --> 00:09:12.960
That means you need to manage the failover

209
00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:16.800
and the recovery, and the security of these bastion hosts.

210
00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:20.130
But the new way now to avoid using bastion host

211
00:09:20.130 --> 00:09:23.490
is to use, as you know, SSM Session Manager

212
00:09:23.490 --> 00:09:25.740
because we can remote control our instances

213
00:09:25.740 --> 00:09:28.102
that have the SSM agent running on them,

214
00:09:28.102 --> 00:09:33.102
without using SSH, which is a better security model.

215
00:09:33.420 --> 00:09:36.780
Okay. And next we have IPv6.

216
00:09:36.780 --> 00:09:39.480
So IPv6, I'll try to explain to you in short,

217
00:09:39.480 --> 00:09:42.870
but all the IPv6 addresses are going to be public.

218
00:09:42.870 --> 00:09:46.830
And the reason why IPvC addresses were included

219
00:09:46.830 --> 00:09:50.190
and inserted and created, is that we are running out

220
00:09:50.190 --> 00:09:52.350
of IPv4 addresses.

221
00:09:52.350 --> 00:09:56.160
So IPv4 addresses are a total of 4.3 billion,

222
00:09:56.160 --> 00:09:57.900
but currently we're about to run out.

223
00:09:57.900 --> 00:10:02.550
And so we have invented IPv6 to have public addresses

224
00:10:02.550 --> 00:10:05.160
and we have a total of 3.4 times 10

225
00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:09.780
at the power of 38 addresses, which is a huge number, okay?

226
00:10:09.780 --> 00:10:13.380
So here is an example of a CIDR for an IPv6,

227
00:10:13.380 --> 00:10:15.540
it looks a lot more complicated than IPv4,

228
00:10:15.540 --> 00:10:18.600
you are not expected to know how this works exactly,

229
00:10:18.600 --> 00:10:19.980
but this is the same idea,

230
00:10:19.980 --> 00:10:22.140
on the left hand side, there is an IPv6,

231
00:10:22.140 --> 00:10:25.080
and on the right hand side there is a subnet mask.

232
00:10:25.080 --> 00:10:27.180
The addresses are seemingly random,

233
00:10:27.180 --> 00:10:29.450
and the idea is that because there's so many addresses

234
00:10:29.450 --> 00:10:33.960
online that can be IPv6, then no attacker can scan them all,

235
00:10:33.960 --> 00:10:36.030
it will take forever, and so the idea is

236
00:10:36.030 --> 00:10:37.710
that if you're using an IPv6,

237
00:10:37.710 --> 00:10:40.140
your instance is somewhat going to be protected

238
00:10:40.140 --> 00:10:43.920
from attacks as long as people can't guess its IP address.

239
00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:46.080
So this is quite a beautiful thing,

240
00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:49.230
and the internet is slowly transitioning

241
00:10:49.230 --> 00:10:51.600
from IPv4 to IPv6.

242
00:10:51.600 --> 00:10:54.690
Now, your VPC does have support for IPv6.

243
00:10:54.690 --> 00:10:55.620
How does it work?

244
00:10:55.620 --> 00:10:58.620
We can create a IPv6 CIDR for your VPC

245
00:10:58.620 --> 00:11:00.180
like the one I showed you above,

246
00:11:00.180 --> 00:11:03.060
and then you use an internet gateway, which as I said,

247
00:11:03.060 --> 00:11:04.920
supports IPv6 traffic.

248
00:11:04.920 --> 00:11:06.390
So, how to make this work?

249
00:11:06.390 --> 00:11:08.130
Well, in your public subnet,

250
00:11:08.130 --> 00:11:09.990
you're going to create an instance

251
00:11:09.990 --> 00:11:13.110
with IPv6 support, and that IPv6 will correspond

252
00:11:13.110 --> 00:11:16.230
to the CIDR that has been assigned to your VPC.

253
00:11:16.230 --> 00:11:19.050
And then you have to create a route table entry

254
00:11:19.050 --> 00:11:23.670
for ::/0, which is the shorthand of IPv6 also,

255
00:11:23.670 --> 00:11:28.670
for IPv4 was 0 0 0/0, and for IPv6 it's ::/0,

256
00:11:29.310 --> 00:11:31.726
which represents all IPv6, and so we're saying

257
00:11:31.726 --> 00:11:35.460
any traffic to any IPv6 is going to be sent

258
00:11:35.460 --> 00:11:38.040
to the internet gateway, which, remember,

259
00:11:38.040 --> 00:11:39.420
it supports IPv6.

260
00:11:39.420 --> 00:11:42.180
And so for your instances in private subnets,

261
00:11:42.180 --> 00:11:44.460
they're still private so they still don't have an IPv4

262
00:11:44.460 --> 00:11:46.170
and they still don't have an IPv6,

263
00:11:46.170 --> 00:11:48.900
but they may want to target external services

264
00:11:48.900 --> 00:11:51.300
that are only reachable using IPv6,

265
00:11:51.300 --> 00:11:53.070
in which case, we have to create something

266
00:11:53.070 --> 00:11:55.650
very similar to a NAT Gateway, but for IPv6

267
00:11:55.650 --> 00:11:58.470
which is called an Egress-Only internet gateway,

268
00:11:58.470 --> 00:12:01.410
in your VPC, and this has support only

269
00:12:01.410 --> 00:12:04.590
for IPv6, and then we had a route table entry

270
00:12:04.590 --> 00:12:09.390
from the private subnet for ::/0, so all IPv6,

271
00:12:09.390 --> 00:12:12.720
directly into the Egress-Only internet gateway,

272
00:12:12.720 --> 00:12:15.540
and this way our instances in our private subnets

273
00:12:15.540 --> 00:12:19.290
will have IPv6 support to reach to external services.

274
00:12:19.290 --> 00:12:20.160
And that's it.

275
00:12:20.160 --> 00:12:22.620
So all of this should be something you seemingly know

276
00:12:22.620 --> 00:12:25.080
for the VPC, those are why they're called the basics,

277
00:12:25.080 --> 00:12:27.360
and then in the next lecture we're going to view

278
00:12:27.360 --> 00:12:30.150
a lot more details on a few aspects of VPC

279
00:12:30.150 --> 00:12:32.670
that are going to be critical going into the exam.

280
00:12:32.670 --> 00:12:33.503
So that's it.

281
00:12:33.503 --> 00:12:35.110
I will see you in the next lecture.

